  <section id="frame-math" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Work frame math, worked through</h2>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 520 260" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Tilted work frame" style="max-width:520px;font:12px system-ui">
<defs><marker id="wfa" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>
<g stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="1.2"><path d="M40 220H480" opacity=".4"/><path d="M60 220V40" opacity=".4"/>
<path d="M60 220L260 220L260 160L60 160Z" opacity=".5"/><path d="M260 160L340 220" stroke-width="2"/>
<path d="M300 190L355 135" marker-end="url(#wfa)" stroke="#3b82f6"/><path d="M300 190L355 245" marker-end="url(#wfa)" stroke="#ef4444"/></g>
<g fill="currentColor"><circle cx="300" cy="190" r="3"/><text x="365" y="132" fill="#3b82f6">z = (0.707, 0, 0.707)</text><text x="365" y="250" fill="#ef4444">x = (0.707, 0, −0.707)</text>
<text x="200" y="180" text-anchor="end">origin (40, 0, 10)</text><text x="470" y="215" text-anchor="end" opacity=".6">world X</text><text x="66" y="50" opacity=".6">world Z</text></g></svg>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Build, check and use a 45° frame">
{"import { cam } from \u0022@cadcamfun/core\u0022\n\nconst frame = cam.frameFromNormal({ x: 40, y: 0, z: 10 }, { x: 1, y: 0, z: 1 })\n// xAxis (0.707, 0, -0.707)  yAxis (0, 1, 0)  zAxis (0.707, 0, 0.707)\ncam.validateWorkFrame(frame)                     // []\n\nconst world = cam.workToWorld(frame, { x: 10, y: 5, z: 0 })   // (47.071, 5, 2.929)\ncam.worldToWork(frame, world)                    // (10, 5, 0)\n\ncam.validateWorkFrame({ origin: { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 },\n  xAxis: { x: 1, y: 0, z: 0 }, yAxis: { x: 0, y: 0, z: 1 }, zAxis: { x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 } })\n// [{ field: \u0022frame\u0022, message: \u0022Work frame axes must be orthonormal and right-handed\u0022 }]"}
    </DocCode>
    <code>frameFromNormal</code> takes the normal as Z and projects the X hint (world X by default) onto the plane. When the hint is parallel to the normal it picks another perpendicular. Y is always Z × X, so the result is right-handed by construction.
  </section>

  <section id="rotary-table" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Rotary solutions for common faces</h2>
    <code>anglesFor(normal, layout, limits)</code> returns every way the table can bring a part direction to machine +Z, smallest tilt first, with the tilt inside the limits (default −120° to 120°). C is wrapped into (−180°, 180°]. These are real outputs:
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Part normal</th><th>table-AC</th><th>table-BC</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>(1, 0, 1), 45° side</td><td>A45 C90, then A−45 C−90</td><td>B−45 C0, then B45 C180</td></tr>
        <tr><td>(1, 0, 0), +X face</td><td>A90 C90, then A−90 C−90</td><td>B−90 C0, then B90 C180</td></tr>
        <tr><td>(0, −1, 0), front face</td><td>A90 C180, then A−90 C0</td><td>B−90 C90, then B90 C−90</td></tr>
        <tr><td>(0, 0, −1), underside</td><td>none (empty array)</td><td>none (empty array)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>An underside needs a 180° tilt, outside the default limits, so the list is empty and <code>indexToolpath</code> throws "The machine's rotary axes cannot reach this work plane within their limits". Machine the underside in a second setup instead.</p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Where the frame origin lands after indexing">
{"const [angles] = cam.anglesFor({ x: 1, y: 0, z: 1 }, \u0022table-BC\u0022)   // { b: -45, c: 0 }\ncam.partToMachine({ x: 40, y: 0, z: 10 }, angles, \u0022table-BC\u0022, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 })\n// (21.213, 0, 35.355): the origin rotated -45\u00b0 about Y around the pivot"}
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="setup-validation" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Setup validation messages</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Everything wrong at once">
{"cam.validateSetup({\n  id: \u0022\u0022, revision: -1, frame,\n  stock: { min: { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 }, max: { x: 0, y: 10, z: 10 } },\n  fixtures: [],\n  tools: [{ toolId: \u0022t\u0022, cuttingDiameter: 6, cuttingLength: 30, stickout: 20,\n            holderDiameter: 32, holderLength: 40 }],\n})"}
    </DocCode>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>field</th><th>message</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>Setup ID must not be empty</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>revision</code></td><td>Setup revision must be a non-negative safe integer</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>stock</code></td><td>Envelope must have finite coordinates and positive volume</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tools.t</code></td><td>Cutting length must not exceed stickout</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Issues are returned, not thrown, and all are reported in one pass, so a form can mark every bad field together.</p>
  </section>

